<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crnojević noble family</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Crnojević dynasty, Crnojevići</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Crnojević family ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, the core of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, in the fifteenth century. Rising after the decline of the Balšić lords, they led the highland clans between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-crnojevic">Ivan Crnojević</a> moved the seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a> in 1482, and his son Đurađ printed the first Cyrillic books among the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. The Ottomans absorbed their lands in the 1490s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crnojevic-noble-family.jpg" alt="Crnojević noble family" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Медун 1876 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_Crnojevi%C4%87_dynasty.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origins of the family are obscure. Its earliest known forebear is Đuraš Vrančić, a nobleman who served King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Stefan Milutin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> (r. 1282–1321). His grandson Đuraš Ilijić, a military leader under Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>, was head of Upper Zeta from 1326 until 1362, when the family was still called Đurašević. In 1355 Dušan sent him to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skradin">Skradin</a>, a Croatian city of his sister Jelena Nemanjić Šubić under Hungarian attack. The sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-i">Balša I</a> killed him as a rival in 1362.</p><p>The Balšić family then greatly suppressed the Đuraševići, who kept to Budva and the Gulf of Kotor. The descendants of Crnoje Đurašević, a son of Đuraš, came to bear the name Crnojević. Crnoje's son Radič, master of Budva at the end of the 14th century, was an honorary citizen of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, threatened Kotor and added Grbalj and Paštrovići to his domain, until he was killed in 1396 in a war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsic-noble-family">House of Balšić</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1403 Radič's sons Đurađ and Aleksa ruled Upper Zeta as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. Stefan I, called Stefanica (1451–1465), used the turmoil in Zeta to become its de facto ruler and married Mara, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjon-kastrioti">Gjon Kastrioti</a>. After southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> fell to the Turks in 1455, he acknowledged Venetian rule in return for autonomy in internal affairs and for the Orthodox Metropolitan.</p><p>His son Ivan (1465–1490), better known as Ivan-beg, began with a war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> but turned back to it in the face of the Turkish threat. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Upper Zeta in 1474 and his lands around Žabljak in 1478. He returned in 1481, after the death of Mehmet the Conqueror, as an Ottoman vassal, keeping the coast above Kotor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-skadar">Lake Skadar</a>, and moved the seat of the Zeta Metropolitan from Prevlaka to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a>, which became his capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ivan's son Đurađ (1490–1496) founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a> monastery, where the first book in the Cyrillic script of Church Slavonic among the South Slavs was printed in 1494. When his contacts with the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> about an anti-Ottoman war became known to the Turks, he had to flee, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> fell under direct Turkish rule. His brother Stefan II then led its administration for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> until 1499, and another brother, Staniša, islamised and renamed Skender-beg, ruled the ancestral lands from 1514 to 1528. Đurađ's descendants lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and died out in 1660.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Crnojević noble family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the last <b>Crnojević</b> rulers left Zeta in the 1490s and the land was absorbed into the Ottoman sanjak of Scutari, the metropolitans of Cetinje became the recognised leaders of the highland clans; their secular role is traditionally dated from 1516.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-montenegro">Prince-Bishopric of Montenegro</a></li><li><blockquote>Venice kept the coastal towns, and the inland region later became the Principality of Zeta under the <b>Crnojević</b> family.</blockquote><a href="/state/zeta-under-the-balsici">Zeta under the Balšići</a></li><li><blockquote>After the decline of the Balšić lords and the Serbian Despotate, the <b>Crnojević</b> family took the lead in the mountains of Zeta.</blockquote><a href="/state/zeta-under-the-crnojevici">Zeta under the Crnojevići</a></li></ul></section>
